description:
Write the document model behind lesson 6.01's editor: the text, edits, multi-level undo and redo with typing grouped into words, the unsaved-changes flag, line and column, find, and Replace All as a single undo step. A text view (an `EDIT` control replacement drawn with DirectWrite, lesson 4.03) then only displays it and turns keys into calls.

```cpp
struct LineCol { int line, col; };    // both 1-based

class TextDocument
{
public:
    explicit TextDocument(std::string text = "");
    const std::string& text() const;

    void insert(std::size_t pos, const std::string& s);
    void erase(std::size_t pos, std::size_t count);
    void replace(std::size_t pos, std::size_t count, const std::string& s);
    void breakGroup();
    bool undo();
    bool redo();

    bool modified() const;
    void markSaved();

    LineCol lineCol(std::size_t pos) const;
    std::size_t offsetOf(int line, int col) const;
    std::optional<std::size_t> find(const std::string& what, std::size_t from, bool matchCase, bool wrap) const;
    int replaceAll(const std::string& what, const std::string& with, bool matchCase);
};
```

**Edits and undo**: positions beyond the end are clamped to the end (and counts to what is there). Every edit that changes something becomes an undo step and clears the redo list. `replace` is one step. `undo` reverts the last step and `redo` re-applies it; both return false when there is nothing to do.

**Typing groups**: an `insert` of a **single character** joins the previous step instead of making a new one when:
- the previous step was also made by `insert` and nothing has broken the group since (`breakGroup`, `undo`, `redo`, `markSaved` or any other kind of edit break it),
- it continues exactly where the previous step's text ended,
- the character is not `\n`, and the previous step does not end with a space unless this character is a space too (so a word and the spaces after it form one step).

**Modified** is true when the text differs from the last saved (or initial) state as far as undo can tell: track the position in the undo history.

**lineCol** counts lines from 1, starting after each `\n`, and columns from 1 (in bytes). **offsetOf** clamps the line to the existing lines and the column to `1 .. length of the line + 1` (the line's length excludes its `\n`).

**find** searches forward from `from`, ignoring case unless `matchCase`; with `wrap`, if nothing is found from `from`, it searches again from the start. An empty `what` finds nothing. **replaceAll** replaces every non-overlapping match from left to right, as **one** undo step, and returns how many it replaced (0 changes nothing and adds no step).

